\section{Resolve Page References Against the Last Stable Layout}

A structured document may contain links whose visible wording includes the destination page number. The anchor identity can be resolved before typesetting, but the page number is a property of pagination. For an illustrative layout workflow, assume anchors have unique identifiers and that pagination is deterministic for fixed metrics, style, and reference strings. The inputs are the document, a placeholder string, and a pass budget. The algorithm tries successive assignments of displayed page strings. It returns a layout whose references agree with its own anchor map, or a diagnostic saying the iterative process did not settle. The process is not a description of a particular historical typesetter.

\begin{verbatim}
layout_refs(doc, placeholder, budget):
  labels = initial_labels(doc, placeholder)
  seen = empty set
  repeat at most budget times:
    if labels in seen: return LayoutCycle
    add labels to seen
    # Pagination records anchors by identity.
    pages = paginate(doc, labels)
    if missing_target(doc, pages): return UnresolvedAnchor
    next = format_target_pages(doc, pages)
    # The returned pages must match the strings they used.
    if next == labels: return pages
    labels = next
  return LayoutBudgetExceeded
\end{verbatim}

In a fixture, an entry's anchor is on page \(9\) when a cross-reference is rendered as \texttt{?}. Formatting it as \texttt{9} pushes a preceding line over its width, and the anchor moves to page \(10\). A further pass with \texttt{10} leaves the anchor on page \(10\). The strings tried are thus \texttt{?}, \texttt{9}, \texttt{10}, and the returned layout displays \texttt{10} for an anchor on page \(10\). Returning after one formatting pass would display a stale number.

After each unsuccessful pass, every proposed label comes from the anchor map just computed; only a fixed point makes the map and displayed labels simultaneously valid. With deterministic inputs, a repeated label assignment proves a cycle in this iteration, not the absence of all possible layouts. Fixed-width page-number boxes with enough digits can sometimes remove the feedback by making pagination independent of the chosen numbers. Recomputing the page map is still necessary when a font, format, or geometry changes.
